Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)
Geriatric & Frailty
Weak/intermittent urinary stream, Nocturia/urinary frequency, Symmetrically enlarged smooth prostate, Increased post-void residual

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-002
Reason for Visit: Acute-on-chronic lower urinary tract symptoms with severe lower abdominal pain and suspected impending urinary retention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe suprapubic pain accompanied by an inability to pass a meaningful volume of urine for the past 6 hours. This required urgent clinical assessment to rule out acute complete urinary retention and prevent bladder injury or post-renal acute kidney injury prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower stomach is in agony, I desperately need to pee but only a few drops are coming out no matter how hard I push."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a progressive hesitancy with delayed initiation of micturition over the past year. Over the last 24 hours, this progressed to a weak, intermittent, or completely interrupted urinary stream. He notes a sensation of incomplete bladder emptying immediately following voiding and has resorted to straining and the intense use of abdominal musculature required to pass urine. Earlier today, terminal dribbling at the end of micturition worsened. He confirms increased urinary frequency occurring predominantly during the day and significant nocturia leading to severe sleep disruption over the past week. Three hours ago, he experienced sudden, compelling urinary urgency occasionally resulting in urge incontinence, which has now transitioned into acute suprapubic pain and an inability to void.
Functional Status: Independent, currently pacing the room in significant discomfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, high risk of falls during frequent nocturnal bathroom trips.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 160/95 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Pacing the room, visibly uncomfortable, frequently holding the lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals a visibly distended and exquisitely tender suprapubic region, dull to percussion, consistent with a distended bladder. Digital rectal examination reveals a symmetrically enlarged, smooth, firm, and non-tender prostate gland with an obliterated median sulcus. Bladder percussion and palpation are consistent with an increased post-void residual volume. The patient demonstrates a weak/intermittent urinary stream when attempting to void in the bathroom. Nocturia/urinary frequency is evident from the history and visible fatigue.
Environmental Observations: Trip hazards noted on the path from the bedroom to the bathroom, specifically loose rugs.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient recently started an over-the-counter oral decongestant (pseudoephedrine) for a cold, which likely precipitated the acute retention.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH) with threatened acute urinary retention - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, rigors, or flank pain, and initial bedside urinalysis of the small passed volume is negative for nitrites and leukocytes.

Prostate Cancer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, smooth, and firm nature of the prostate on digital rectal exam without discrete hard nodules or fixed asymmetry.

Nephrolithiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of unilateral colicky flank pain radiating to the groin; pain is exclusively midline and suprapubic.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute suprapubic pain, severe straining, and near-complete cessation of urine flow, indicating threatened or early acute urinary retention secondary to BPH. Telephone triage accurately identified the risk of progressive urinary retention. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period carried a high, unacceptable risk of total urinary obstruction, subsequent acute kidney injury, severe pain crisis, or spontaneous bladder rupture. Urgent after-hours assessment was definitively required to evaluate bladder volume, cease offending precipitant medications, initiate alpha-blocker therapy, and determine if emergency catheterization was immediately warranted, successfully averting irreversible organ damage and an emergency department admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced tamsulosin 0.4mg daily to relax prostatic smooth muscle. Directed immediate cessation of over-the-counter pseudoephedrine. Facilitated a voiding trial post-hydration which yielded 200mL, relieving acute capsular stretch, though residual volume remains. Ordered formal uroflowmetry and renal tract ultrasound for the primary care follow-up.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a scheduled fluid intake and voiding routine. Step 1: The patient consumes daily fluids before 6:00 PM and attempts double-voiding (urinating, waiting two minutes, and urating again) before bed. Step 2: Upon successful completion of a dry, uninterrupted 4-hour sleep block, the patient rewards themselves with a preferred specialty coffee in the morning. Cause-and-effect: The morning reward reinforces the adherence to evening fluid restriction and double-voiding, systematically decreasing nocturnal bladder distension and sleep disruption.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated at this time.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urgently recommended removal of loose rugs and installation of a nightlight in the hallway.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 24 hours to reassess bladder emptying and monitor renal function.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the patient becomes completely unable to pass urine for a continuous 6-hour period, develops unbearable abdominal pain, experiences gross hematuria (blood in urine), or develops a fever with chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis is presumptive and requires confirmation via formal uroflowmetry and ultrasound to rule out other obstructive pathologies.
Clinical Course: Urinary flow should slowly improve over the next 3 to 5 days as the new medication takes effect and the cold medication washes out of the system. Complete retention remains a risk in the immediate short term.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you cannot pass any urine for 6 hours and your lower stomach becomes hard and intensely painful. See your regular GP tomorrow for a formal scan of your bladder.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The new medication works by relaxing the muscle fibers in the prostate and the neck of the bladder, widening the channel to let urine flow more easily. The cold medication you took had the exact opposite effect, clamping the channel shut.
Actionable Steps: Stop all decongestants. Drink your fluids during the day and stop at 6 PM. Practice double-voiding before bed, and treat yourself to your favorite morning coffee when you successfully follow this routine.

